# The variable-height scene (docs/virtualization-plan.md §5): the
# correction path, driven on purpose. guests/python/varied.py stamps 300
# rows whose heights differ by STRUCTURE — row i holds an inner For of
# k lines, k = 1 + (i * 7 % 11) % 5 — so the same row is the same number
# of lines on every platform while its pixel height is that platform's
# business. Row 0 is one line and row 1 is three, so the presumption
# taken from the first realized row fails at the second and the For is
# on the corrected path for the rest of its life (plan §2).
#
# WHAT THIS SCENE MAY ASSERT, and what it must not: the correction
# changes pixel arithmetic, and pixel extents on this path are
# legitimately platform- and history-dependent. So every expectation
# below is a CORRECTION-STABLE fact — band membership by row identity,
# the first index after each move, the declared total, one row's own
# inner content — and none is an extent.
#
# expect_window says the FIRST VISIBLE row and the DECLARED TOTAL,
# both data (ruled 2026-08-25); the band's WIDTH is the viewport's
# business and the compiled table-tier gate's to hold, so no line here
# names it.
#
# ONE ROW'S CONTENT IS FAIR GAME, though the band's listing is not: each
# row's inner For carries that row's key as its automation id, so
# `expect_order column@r200` reads exactly one copy's lines and says
# nothing about how many copies exist. That is what proves nested
# collection instances outlive their stamps (ruled 2026-08-25 — the
# entry it closed is in docs/deferred.md, the measurement in
# docs/traps.md).
expect label#0 "300 rows, 845 lines"
# The whole collection is DATA — 300 rows declared — while a band of
# widgets exists. THE SENTENCE: a windowed For's total is what the
# guest inserted, not what the platform realized.
expect_window column@varied 0 300
# THE INNER LIST OF A REALIZED ROW. Each row's own For carries the row's
# key as its automation id, so one row's lines read out on their own.
expect_order column@r000 "r000 line 1"
# Deep, past two hundred rows the band has never realized: every
# position it crossed was PRESUMED from row 0's pitch, which is already
# known to be wrong. THE SENTENCE: the scroll follows a ROW, so an
# unrealized row is reachable by identity.
scroll_to_row column@varied r200
expect_window column@varied 200 300
# THE WRITE TO AN OUT-OF-BAND ROW, READ BACK. r200's four lines were
# inserted during the build, when the table was seeded at a screenful
# and r200 had no widgets at all — so these are model data that became
# widgets on realization. THE SENTENCE: a nested collection's instance
# belongs to the ROW, not to its stamp.
expect_order column@r200 "r200 line 1|r200 line 2|r200 line 3|r200 line 4"
# Back to the top. The rows between were realized and measured on the
# way, so the prefix sums above the viewport are CORRECTED now and were
# presumed before. THE SENTENCE: correction moves arithmetic, never
# membership — these two lines are byte-identical to the two above.
scroll_to_row column@varied r000
expect_window column@varied 0 300
# AWAY AND BACK. r000's copy was torn down when the band left it and
# stamped again on the way home; its line came back with it. THE
# SENTENCE: teardown takes widgets and leaves data — this line is
# byte-identical to the one before the deep scroll.
expect_order column@r000 "r000 line 1"
# THE ANCHORING ASSERTION (plan §5's last sentence). Park on r150 with
# the fifty rows above it still presumed; realize them by visiting r100,
# which corrects their heights and therefore r150's pixel position; come
# back to r150. THE SENTENCE: scroll position is anchored to ROW
# IDENTITY, so a correction above the viewport adjusts the scrollbar and
# not the content under the reader's eyes — the parked row is the band's
# anchor again, and the band around it holds the same rows.
scroll_to_row column@varied r150
expect_window column@varied 150 300
scroll_to_row column@varied r100
expect_window column@varied 100 300
scroll_to_row column@varied r150
expect_window column@varied 150 300
